    {
      "time": 1789567117.9069166,
      "type": "msg",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "mentions": [],
      "id": "90b416cf-ac1e-49cd-8b4e-69876f50da11",
      "body": "Here's the full picture, confirmed by reading the actual code (not just the docstrings):\n\n**How the pieces connect**\n\n1. `ds_time_column` (`flare_dataset.py:69-71`) picks a column from the HEK catalog (`start_time`, `peak_time`, or `end_time`) and copies it into a new `ds_index` datetime column.\n2. That `ds_index` is then `merge_asof`'d against the actual Surya data timestamps (governed by `ds_time_tolerance` and `ds_match_direction`) to find the nearest real data frame \u2014 this becomes the **reference timestep** for that sample.\n3. `time_delta_input_minutes` offsets (`helio.py`) are added to that reference timestep to pick the actual input frames: `input_timesteps = reference_timestep + offsets`.\n4. The label (`intensity` \u2192 `normalized_intensity`) is just whatever value sits in that catalog row \u2014 it is **not** re-derived from a time window around `ds_index`. So switching `ds_time_column` changes *which timestamp anchors the input window*, not which flare's intensity is being predicted.\n\n**Your two scenarios \u2014 both are pure YAML edits:**\n\n- **Predict from start_time vs peak_time:** just flip\n  ```yaml\n  ds_time_column: start_time   # or peak_time\n  ```\n  This re-anchors the whole input window to be relative to the flare's start instead of its peak.\n\n- **Change offsets to 2hr/1hr before instead of 1hr/36min before:**\n  ```yaml\n  time_delta_input_minutes: [-120, -60]   # instead of [-60, -36]\n  ```\n  There's no code-enforced requirement that these end in `0` (that \"last entry must be 0\" comment in `helio.py` is a convention for other downstream apps that include a \"now\" frame \u2014 flare_forecast doesn't use one; both offsets here are historical, which is intentional since it's causal/pre-flare prediction).\n\n**One thing to watch:** `model.time_embedding.time_dim` (config line 133) must equal `len(time_delta_input_minutes)`. Since you're keeping 2 offsets in both cases, no change needed there \u2014 but if you ever add/remove an offset, update `time_dim` too or you'll get a mismatch error.\n\nSo yes \u2014 for both edits you described, changing `config_script.yaml` alone is sufficient; no dataset/code changes required.",
      "raw_time": false
    },
    {
      "time": 1789567713.3665361,
