    {
      "body": "The big gap under the title isn't the title \u2014 it's the default figure margins. Cell 21 (`32ff2755`) never calls `tight_layout`, so matplotlib keeps its default `top=0.88`, leaving ~12% of the figure height blank above the axes regardless of `suptitle(y=...)`. Fix it by passing margins directly to `GridSpec`.\n\n**Cell 21 (`32ff2755`) \u2014 the main figure**\n\nReplace **lines 104\u2013106**:\n\n```python\n    panel = 3.4                                    # inches per (square) panel\n    title_in = 0.42                                # inches reserved for suptitle\n    fig_w = panel * (n_inputs + n_lead) + panel * w_ratios[n_inputs]\n    fig_h = panel * n_rows + title_in\n    fig = plt.figure(figsize=(fig_w, fig_h), dpi=90)\n    gs = gridspec.GridSpec(n_rows, n_cols, figure=fig,\n                           width_ratios=w_ratios,\n                           wspace=0.02, hspace=0.03,\n                           left=0.004, right=0.996,\n                           top=1 - title_in / fig_h,   # <- kills the title gap\n                           bottom=0.004)\n```\n\nDeriving `fig_h` from `panel * n_rows` is what removes the *interior* whitespace: `imshow` forces a square aspect, so if the axes box isn't square the image gets letterboxed inside it. Matching figure height to width per panel makes each box square and the padding disappears.\n\nReplace **line 134** (the diff-panel title) \u2014 an `set_title` reserves a horizontal band per diff row; moving it inside the panel reclaims it:\n\n```python\n            ax.text(0.01, 0.99, title, transform=ax.transAxes, ha=\"left\", va=\"top\",\n                    fontsize=7, color=\"k\",\n                    bbox=dict(fc=\"w\", ec=\"none\", alpha=0.65, pad=1.5))\n```\n\n**Line 136** \u2014 tighten the colorbar:\n\n```python\n            fig.colorbar(im, ax=ax, fraction=0.040, pad=0.012)\n```\n\n**Line 142** \u2014 pin the title to the strip you reserved:\n\n```python\n        fontsize=10, y=0.999, va=\"top\")\n```\n\n**Line 129** \u2014 the diff row's inner spacing:\n\n```python\n            1, 4, width_ratios=[0.5, 1, 1, 0.5], wspace=0.08)\n```\n\n**Line 100** \u2014 the input\u2194ground-truth gap. You asked for that separation earlier, so tune rather than remove; `0.18` \u2192 `0.10` keeps it visible but tighter:\n\n```python\nw_ratios = [1] * n_inputs + [0.10] + [1] * n_lead\n```\n\nDo **not** add `plt.tight_layout()` to this cell \u2014 it would discard the explicit `top`/`left`/`right`/`bottom` and reintroduce the gap.\n\n**Cells 15 and 20 (`a7f3372e`, `4e578227`) \u2014 the older single-row plots**\n\nThese have the opposite problem: `plt.tight_layout()` on line 58 runs *after* `suptitle`, and older matplotlib doesn't account for the suptitle, so it squeezes the axes and leaves a gap. Edit:\n\n- **Line 23** \u2014 add margins: `gs = gridspec.GridSpec(1, n_cols, figure=fig, wspace=0.03, hspace=0, left=0.006, right=0.994, top=0.90, bottom=0.01)`\n- **Line 22** \u2014 `figsize=np.array([4 * n_cols, 4])` \u2192 `figsize=(3.6 * n_cols, 3.6 + 0.4)` so panels stay square\n- **Line 57** \u2014 `y=0.995, va=\"top\"`\n- **Line 58** \u2014 delete `plt.tight_layout()`\n\n**Cell 19 (`bf142f1b`) \u2014 the diff pair**\n\n- **Line 22** \u2014 `figsize=(9, 4.5)` \u2192 `figsize=(8.4, 4.3)`\n- **Line 32** \u2014 `ax.set_title(title, fontsize=7, pad=3)`\n- **Line 34** \u2014 `fig.colorbar(im, ax=ax, fraction=0.040, pad=0.015)`\n- **Line 36** \u2014 `plt.tight_layout(pad=0.4, w_pad=0.3)`\n\nIf you'd rather not hand-tune margins at all, the one-line alternative for any of these is to swap `plt.figure(...)` for `plt.figure(..., layout=\"constrained\")` and delete the `tight_layout()` call \u2014 then set `fig.get_layout_engine().set(h_pad=0.01, w_pad=0.01, hspace=0.01, wspace=0.01)`. It handles the suptitle correctly, but gives you less exact control over the centered diff rows than the explicit margins above.",
