    {
      "id": "54bfb4fa-ece1-4d51-8a14-d779e2666809",
      "body": "Found it \u2014 I can see from the saved output that the cell ran clean (no error), produced both figures, and the timestamps/paths all check out. So \"messed up\" is visual, and I'm fairly sure I know the cause: **I coupled the diff color limit to the same `factor` as the images, but for diffs that knob works backwards.**\n\nLine 144 is currently:\n```python\n            lim = np.percentile(np.abs(d), 99) * factor or 1.0\n```\n\nFor a normal image, a bigger `factor` raises `vmax` \u2192 dimmer. For a symmetric diff, a bigger `factor` widens the range \u2192 **washed out**, and a smaller one \u2192 **saturated solid red/blue blobs**. Your original cell 19 used a fixed `*10`. So my version gives you:\n\n- `\"normal\"` (factor=1) \u2192 limit 10\u00d7 tighter than what you had \u2192 blown-out blobs\n- `\"enhanced\"` (factor=10) \u2192 same as your old cell 19\n\ni.e. the diff row you were happy with got replaced by a saturated one. The fix is to give diffs their own gain, independent of the image scaling.\n\n**Line 11** \u2014 add a third element per version:\n```python\nSCALE_VERSIONS   = [(\"normal\", 1, 10), (\"enhanced\", 10, 3)]  # (tag, image_factor, diff_gain)\n```\n\n**Line 106** \u2014 unpack it:\n```python\nfor tag, factor, diff_gain in SCALE_VERSIONS:\n```\n\n**Line 144** \u2014 replace that one line with three:\n```python\n            ad = np.abs(d)\n            nz = ad[ad != 0]              # off-disk pixels are exactly 0 in both frames\n            lim = (np.percentile(nz, 99) * diff_gain) if nz.size else 1.0\n```\n\n**Line 153** (optional, so the figure tells you which knobs produced it):\n```python\n        f\"[{tag}: img 99pct \u00d7 {factor}, diff \u00d7 {diff_gain}]   {row['GOES_class']}   \"\n```\n\nThe mental model to keep: for the **images**, higher factor = dimmer. For the **diffs**, higher `diff_gain` = calmer/subtler. So if the diffs are still too faint, *lower* `diff_gain` (try 3 \u2192 1.5); if they're solid blobs, raise it.\n\nMasking the zeros also matters here \u2014 a large fraction of every frame is off-disk and exactly 0 in both, which drags the 99th percentile down and over-saturates the on-disk signal. That's what the `nz` line fixes.",
      "raw_time": false,
      "type": "msg",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "mentions": [],
      "time": 1789570626.026971
    },
