    {
      "body": "The three edits above (lines 11, 106, 144) are the fix if the diffs are over-saturated or washed out \u2014 which is what I'd expect given the scaling regression.\n\nOne thing I couldn't verify: I can read the notebook's saved PNG metadata but not the rendered pixels, so if the actual symptom is **layout** rather than scaling \u2014 diff panels misaligned or smaller than the grid above \u2014 the cause is different. That would be the `fig.colorbar(...)` on line 150 stealing width from the axes, which `imshow`'s forced square aspect then shrinks the whole panel to compensate. Fix for that case:\n\n```python\n            cax = ax.inset_axes([1.02, 0.0, 0.03, 1.0])\n            fig.colorbar(im, cax=cax)\n```\n\nThat puts the colorbar outside the axes box so the diff panels stay the same size as the image panels above.\n\nLet me know which symptom you're seeing and I'll narrow it down \u2014 or paste the figure back and I can look at it directly.",
