      "body": "the image formatting getting messed up: what i men by that is that its not human readable and has too much space between the images and its all very small and kind of squished. coe cell 22 how can i make that 2x2 grid bigger ike the 2x3 grid that comes before it? also i dont want space between the different grid outputs, at least not much space ",
      "id": "fcfa6121-5b52-4b99-9cb1-e0abfe99449d"
    },
    {
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "802d3e83-c3a1-4f27-a74f-a5678ee63f49",
      "time": 1789570872.1590347,
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "I measured the rendered PNG directly, and the numbers confirm exactly what you're describing:\n\n| band | y-range | height |\n|---|---|---|\n| suptitle | 9\u201321 | 13 px |\n| image row 1 (aia131) | 45\u2013344 | **300 px** |\n| image row 2 (aia171) | 352\u2013651 | **300 px** |\n| diff row 1 | 719\u2013898 | **180 px** |\n| diff row 2 | 1026\u20131205 | **180 px** |\n\nSo the diff panels render at **180 px vs 300 px** for the image panels, and there's **68 px** of dead space above the first diff row and **128 px** between the two diff rows.\n\n**Root cause:** my centering trick on line 141, `subgridspec(1, 4, width_ratios=[0.5, 1, 1, 0.5], wspace=0.18)`, allots each diff panel only ~1/3 of the row *minus* an 18% `wspace` gap, *minus* what `fig.colorbar(..., fraction=0.040)` steals. `imshow` forces a square aspect, so the panel shrinks to match that narrowed **width** \u2014 180 px \u2014 and the leftover 107 px of row height becomes the gaps you're seeing. The gaps aren't a spacing setting; they're slack from undersized panels.\n\n**Fix: give the diff panels exactly the same 1-unit width as the image panels.**\n\n**Line 104** \u2014 currently `[0.18]`:\n```python\nw_ratios = [1] * n_inputs + [0.10] + [1] * n_lead\n```\n\n**Lines 140\u2013141** \u2014 replace the `inner = ...` statement:\n```python\n        # Match the image rows exactly: [margin, panel, gap, panel, margin] sums to\n        # 0.5 + 1 + 0.10 + 1 + 0.5 = 3.10 units = the parent row width (3 panels + spacer).\n        # wspace=0 is essential -- any wspace shrinks the panels below 1 unit again.\n        gap_u = w_ratios[n_inputs]\n        side  = (sum(w_ratios) - (2 + gap_u)) / 2\n        inner = gs[len(CHANNELS_TO_PLOT) + r, :].subgridspec(\n            1, 5, width_ratios=[side, 1, gap_u, 1, side], wspace=0)\n```\n\n**Line 143** \u2014 the diff panels now live in inner columns 1 and 3:\n```python\n            ax = fig.add_subplot(inner[0, 1 + 2 * k])\n```\n\n**Line 150** \u2014 the colorbar must stop stealing width from the axes, or the panel shrinks again:\n```python\n            cax = ax.inset_axes([1.015, 0.0, 0.022, 1.0])\n            cb = fig.colorbar(im, cax=cax)\n            cb.ax.tick_params(labelsize=6)\n```\n\nThat alone makes all four diff panels 300 px like the image rows, and collapses the 68/128 px gaps to the uniform `hspace=0.03` you already set on line 115.\n\n**For crispness**, bump dpi on **line 112**:\n```python\n    fig = plt.figure(figsize=(fig_w, fig_h), dpi=110)\n```\n\nOne caveat about \"make it bigger\" that's worth knowing before you tune `panel` on line 108: Jupyter scales a figure down to fit the output area width, so *increasing* `figsize` past ~1000 px wide makes each panel appear **smaller**, not bigger. Your figure is currently 961 px wide \u2014 right at the boundary. So raising `panel` from 3.4 won't help; what actually buys you visible size is eliminating wasted width, which the fix above does (it reclaims the 32% that the `[0.5, ..., 0.5]` margins were burning).\n\nIf you want the diff panels genuinely *larger* than the image panels, the way to get it is to drop the centering and give the diff grid its own figure with only 2 columns \u2014 each panel then gets ~48% of the width instead of 32%. Say the word and I'll write that variant.\n\nTwo smaller notes:\n- The `diff_gain` fix from my last message isn't in yet \u2014 line 144 still reads `* factor`, so the \"normal\" version's diffs will stay over-saturated. Independent of layout; apply it whenever.\n- The gap *between* the two scaling figures is Jupyter's CSS between output images, not something matplotlib controls. The only way to get zero space is to render both versions as rows of a single figure (8 rows instead of two 4-row figures)."
